  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1: Relatively quiet weather for the rest of the week with some small \nchances of rain.\n\nResidual scattered showers may linger this morning for the western \nmountainous counties but the cold front is moving through and the \nbulk of the precipitation is out of the area. A surface high \npressure system will follow with its center traveling \nsoutheastward into the Atlantic by the weekend. The new air \nmass will support relatively quiet and drier weather for the \nnext few days.\n\nMeanwhile, a large mid-level low in our vicinity will propagate to \nthe northeast and lead to more zonal flow over the Mid-Atlantic. \nSome perturbations will swing by to provide additional chances for \nupslope based precipitation for this weekend. Given the drier air, \nserious lack of synoptic lift, and weak shear, these showers are \nexpected to be limited in coverage and intensity. The zonal flow \nalso means temperatures and dew points will start to recover with \nhighs in the upper 70s to upper 80s by Saturday.\n\n\nKey Message 2: Warming trend next week with abnormally hot\ntemperatures.\n\nMid-level ridging and low level southwesterly winds will promote a \nwarming and humid trend for the region next week. Following the \nprevious forecaster's discussion, the warm temperatures may feel \nlike a shock given the relatively cool weather we have been \nexperiencing. High temperatures are likely to get into the \nupper 80s to lower 90s with dew points climbing into the 50s and\n60s. This trend is likely to persist for the first half of the \nweek so the public is encouraged to prepare for a shift in \nweather conditions. The ending of this trend, however, is a bit \nunclear. Model guidance continues to argue the existence of a \ncold front that forms with a surface low early next week, but it\nis still not clear how far east the front will progress. (Some \nmodels argue it will orient itself zonally and be to our north).\nThe cold front's impact will disrupt the warming trend and give\nanother chance of precipitation if it goes through so this \nsystem will be monitored in future forecasts.",
